            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "gorilla/sessions.NewCookieStore (and NewFilesystemStore) initialise Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteNoneMode by default, which allows the session cookie to be sent on cross-site requests and removes a key CSRF defence. The existing session-cookie-samesitenone rule only catches an explicit &sessions.Options{SameSite: http.SameSiteNoneMode}; this insecure value is also the silent default when you never set Options at all. After NewCookieStore, explicitly set store.Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteLaxMode or http.SameSiteStrictMode."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "gorilla/sessions.NewCookieStore (and NewFilesystemStore) initialise Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteNoneMode by default, which allows the session cookie to be sent on cross-site requests and removes a key CSRF defence. The existing session-cookie-samesitenone rule only catches an explicit &sessions.Options{SameSite: http.SameSiteNoneMode}; this insecure value is also the silent default when you never set Options at all. After NewCookieStore, explicitly set store.Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteLaxMode or http.SameSiteStrictMode.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://github.com/gorilla/sessions/blob/v1.4.0/store.go#L59](https://github.com/gorilla/sessions/blob/v1.4.0/store.go#L59)\n",
                "text": "gorilla/sessions.NewCookieStore (and NewFilesystemStore) initialise Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteNoneMode by default, which allows the session cookie to be sent on cross-site requests and removes a key CSRF defence. The existing session-cookie-samesitenone rule only catches an explicit &sessions.Options{SameSite: http.SameSiteNoneMode}; this insecure value is also the silent default when you never set Options at all. After NewCookieStore, explicitly set store.Options.SameSite to http.SameSiteLaxMode or http.SameSiteStrictMode."
              },
              "id": "auditoria.semgrep-wacrm-2026-09-13.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.gorilla.security.audit.gorilla-cookiestore-default-samesite-none",
              "name": "auditoria.semgrep-wacrm-2026-09-13.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.gorilla.security.audit.gorilla-cookiestore-default-samesite-none",
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-1275: Sensitive Cookie with Improper SameSite Attribute",
                  "LOW CONFIDENCE",
                  "OWASP-A02:2025 - Security Misconfiguration",
                  "OWASP-A05:2021 - Security Misconfiguration",
                  "security"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Semgrep Finding: auditoria.semgrep-wacrm-2026-09-13.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.gorilla.security.audit.gorilla-cookiestore-default-samesite-none"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An HTTP handler decodes the request body as JSON without validating the Content-Type header. Browsers send no CORS preflight for \"simple\" requests using text/plain or application/x-www-form-urlencoded, so a cross-site HTML form can POST a body the server parses as JSON (encoding/json also ignores trailing bytes, so a form's trailing \"=\" doesn't break parsing). With cookie auth this is exploitable as CSRF. Reject requests whose Content-Type is not application/json, and/or wrap handlers with http.CrossOriginProtection (Go 1.25+).\nv2: handler matching widened to cover method receivers, extra parameters (e.g. ctx), and common framework handler signatures (gin, echo, chi/std) \u2014 the std-only two-arg pattern missed real handlers like func (h *T) handleWrite(w, r, ctx) found in production code."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "An HTTP handler decodes the request body as JSON without validating the Content-Type header. Browsers send no CORS preflight for \"simple\" requests using text/plain or application/x-www-form-urlencoded, so a cross-site HTML form can POST a body the server parses as JSON (encoding/json also ignores trailing bytes, so a form's trailing \"=\" doesn't break parsing). With cookie auth this is exploitable as CSRF. Reject requests whose Content-Type is not application/json, and/or wrap handlers with http.CrossOriginProtection (Go 1.25+).\nv2: handler matching widened to cover method receivers, extra parameters (e.g. ctx), and common framework handler signatures (gin, echo, chi/std) \u2014 the std-only two-arg pattern missed real handlers like func (h *T) handleWrite(w, r, ctx) found in production code.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#CrossOriginProtection](https://pkg.go.dev/net/http#CrossOriginProtection)\n - [https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json#Decoder.Decode](https://pkg.go.dev/encoding/json#Decoder.Decode)\n",
                "text": "An HTTP handler decodes the request body as JSON without validating the Content-Type header. Browsers send no CORS preflight for \"simple\" requests using text/plain or application/x-www-form-urlencoded, so a cross-site HTML form can POST a body the server parses as JSON (encoding/json also ignores trailing bytes, so a form's trailing \"=\" doesn't break parsing). With cookie auth this is exploitable as CSRF. Reject requests whose Content-Type is not application/json, and/or wrap handlers with http.CrossOriginProtection (Go 1.25+).\nv2: handler matching widened to cover method receivers, extra parameters (e.g. ctx), and common framework handler signatures (gin, echo, chi/std) \u2014 the std-only two-arg pattern missed real handlers like func (h *T) handleWrite(w, r, ctx) found in production code."
              },
              "id": "auditoria.semgrep-wacrm-2026-09-13.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-decode-without-content-type-check",
              "name": "auditoria.semgrep-wacrm-2026-09-13.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-decode-without-content-type-check",
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-352: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)",
                  "LOW CONFIDENCE",
                  "OWASP-A01:2021 - Broken Access Control",
                  "security"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Semgrep Finding: auditoria.semgrep-wacrm-2026-09-13.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.json-decode-without-content-type-check"
              }
            },
            {
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A narrowing cast of len() is written as a binary length prefix via encoding/binary, then the corresponding payload is emitted. This is the exploitable shape from the msgp/DEF CON 32 class: if the length exceeds the narrow type's max it wraps, the on-wire length field desynchronises from the actual payload, and a downstream parser can be smuggled extra/short data. Bound the length against the type max (or the protocol limit) before writing it, and reject oversized payloads."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "A narrowing cast of len() is written as a binary length prefix via encoding/binary, then the corresponding payload is emitted. This is the exploitable shape from the msgp/DEF CON 32 class: if the length exceeds the narrow type's max it wraps, the on-wire length field desynchronises from the actual payload, and a downstream parser can be smuggled extra/short data. Bound the length against the type max (or the protocol limit) before writing it, and reject oversized payloads.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://media.defcon.org/DEF%20CON%2032/DEF%20CON%2032%20presentations/](https://media.defcon.org/DEF%20CON%2032/DEF%20CON%2032%20presentations/)\n - [https://github.com/tinylib/msgp/blob/v1.5.0/msgp/write.go](https://github.com/tinylib/msgp/blob/v1.5.0/msgp/write.go)\n",
                "text": "A narrowing cast of len() is written as a binary length prefix via encoding/binary, then the corresponding payload is emitted. This is the exploitable shape from the msgp/DEF CON 32 class: if the length exceeds the narrow type's max it wraps, the on-wire length field desynchronises from the actual payload, and a downstream parser can be smuggled extra/short data. Bound the length against the type max (or the protocol limit) before writing it, and reject oversized payloads."
              },
              "id": "auditoria.semgrep-wacrm-2026-09-13.repos.elttam-semgrep-rules.rules-audit.go.lang.security.audit.len-cast-into-binary-length-prefix",
