              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The X-Forwarded-For (XFF) HTTP header field is a common method for identifying the originating\nIP address of a client connecting to a web server through an HTTP proxy or load balancer. Since this header \nis not set by server and can be easily spoofed by the client It may cause injection attacks if `$ADDR`\nis not filtered for properly.\n"
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "The X-Forwarded-For (XFF) HTTP header field is a common method for identifying the originating\nIP address of a client connecting to a web server through an HTTP proxy or load balancer. Since this header \nis not set by server and can be easily spoofed by the client It may cause injection attacks if `$ADDR`\nis not filtered for properly.\n\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-Forwarded-For](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X-Forwarded-For)\n",
                "text": "The X-Forwarded-For (XFF) HTTP header field is a common method for identifying the originating\nIP address of a client connecting to a web server through an HTTP proxy or load balancer. Since this header \nis not set by server and can be easily spoofed by the client It may cause injection attacks if `$ADDR`\nis not filtered for properly.\n"
